# Documentation site toolchain: the INPUT to the lock in docs-requirements.txt.
#
# The four top-level tools are pinned to exact versions RESOLVED from a real install on
# 2026-09-28, not from memory: an earlier attempt pinned mkdocs-material==9.6.24, which does
# not exist and which pip rejected. Pin from what actually installed.
#
# docs-requirements.txt is GENERATED from this file. It locks every package these four pull in
# (Jinja2, Markdown, PyYAML, Pygments and the rest), each with its hashes. The workflow installs
# it with --require-hashes, so nothing resolves fresh on a run and a changed upstream file fails
# the install. It also installs with --only-binary ":all:", so pip refuses source distributions
# and cannot execute setup code at install time. The colon-space in that flag must stay quoted
# in YAML, or the parser reads it as a mapping key.
#
# Edit THIS file, never the .txt. To regenerate the lock, run from the repository root, in Git
# Bash, with Docker running. It resolves on Linux with Python 3.12, the same as the runner, so
# environment markers match CI:
#
#   MSYS_NO_PATHCONV=1 docker run --rm -v "$(pwd -W):/repo" -w /repo \
#     -e CUSTOM_COMPILE_COMMAND="see .github/docs-requirements.in" python:3.12-slim sh -c \
#     'pip install --quiet pip-tools==7.6.1 && pip-compile --quiet --generate-hashes --strip-extras \
#      --allow-unsafe --output-file .github/docs-requirements.txt .github/docs-requirements.in'
#
# Then commit both files together. Add --upgrade to move every locked dependency to its newest
# version allowed by the pins below.
mkdocs==1.6.1
mkdocs-material==9.7.7
mkdocs-git-revision-date-localized-plugin==1.6.0
pymdown-extensions==12.1
